WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:03.035
-Our next guest is
a talented actress

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:05.004
and "New York Times"
best-selling author.

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:07.106
Her latest book,
"Ali's Well That Ends Well:

00:00:07.106 --> 00:00:09.308
Tales of Desperation
and a Little Inspiration,"

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:10.376
is available May 10th.

00:00:10.376 --> 00:00:11.811
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:14.213
our friend Ali Wentworth,
everybody.

00:00:14.213 --> 00:00:16.316
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:32.865 --> 00:00:34.000
It's nice to have you back, Ali.
-It's so nice to be here.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.035
-How are you?
-I'm great.

00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:38.872
I'm actually wearing my
teenage daughter's prom shoes,

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:39.972
and they're a little small.

00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:41.007
-Really?

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:43.442
-So I'm so happy
I got across the walk.

00:00:43.442 --> 00:00:44.611
-Is that a situation where you

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:46.679
have to ask permission
to wear them?

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:48.081
-No. She's at
Brown University right now,

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:50.049
so she doesn't even know
that I'm wearing them.

00:00:50.049 --> 00:00:51.484
-Okay, gotcha.
-Yeah.

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:53.186
-And I'm assuming
you might even be

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:54.687
paying for Brown University,

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.523
so how dare she tell you
what shoes you can wear?

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:57.891
-That's right.
-Yeah.

00:00:57.891 --> 00:00:59.526
-This is first semester
right here.

00:00:59.526 --> 00:01:01.861
Yeah.

00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:05.297
-So are you -- I would imagine
having a daughter in college --

00:01:05.297 --> 00:01:06.933
is it drawing
a distinction between what

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:08.734
college was like
when you were there?

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:11.170
-Let me be very clear
about what college was --

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.872
Well, first of all,
going to college was --

00:01:13.872 --> 00:01:15.774
I was in a beat-up Honda.

00:01:15.774 --> 00:01:19.745
I brought my stinky pillow
from home, a toothbrush,

00:01:19.745 --> 00:01:23.082
maybe some birth control,
and a suitcase,

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:24.116
and that was it.

00:01:24.116 --> 00:01:26.051
My parents didn't go with me.

00:01:26.051 --> 00:01:27.687
We didn't go to
Bed bath &amp; Beyond

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:30.290
and get a shower caddy
and all this...

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:31.523
-Yeah.

00:01:31.523 --> 00:01:34.126
-My daughters have
Pinterest boards

00:01:34.126 --> 00:01:36.161
of what they want
their dorm room to look like.

00:01:36.161 --> 00:01:37.730
I'm like, "Are you..."

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.166
It's like 5x7 underground.

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:41.633
You've got some, you know,

00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:43.369
escaped-convict roommate.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:44.504
There's nothing
you can do about it.

00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:45.638
-Yeah.

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:47.807
-Like, we are
spoiling our children.

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:50.042
It should be
uncomfortable and hard.

00:01:50.042 --> 00:01:51.977
-Yeah. It's true.

00:01:51.977 --> 00:01:53.179
It's -- I will say,

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:56.616
I'm pretty sure I was
sent with a shower caddy.

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.451
And then,
when my parents did visit,

00:01:58.451 --> 00:02:00.486
it was, like,
still in its plastic wrapper,

00:02:00.486 --> 00:02:02.121
and they were like,
"Have you not --"

00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:05.457
They were like,
"How have you been caddying --

00:02:05.457 --> 00:02:07.227
How have you been caddying
your shampoo?"

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:09.595
-How do you carry your shampoo
into the shower?

00:02:09.595 --> 00:02:12.098
No, she -- "I have to get, like,
Nike shower shoes."

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:13.633
No, you don't.

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:16.135
There's no fungus that's going
to take over your feet.

00:02:16.135 --> 00:02:19.138
-Yeah. And kids could use
a little fungus.

00:02:19.138 --> 00:02:21.173
-Well, I think boys can.
-Boys can use a little fungus.

00:02:21.173 --> 00:02:22.474
-Boys can use
some little fungus.

00:02:22.474 --> 00:02:24.177
-I mean, I can put
my feet anywhere now.

00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:25.611
Literally anywhere.
-Oh, I see. Yes.

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:27.413
-And I'm totally -- I'm fine.

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:28.681
-Yes. Yes.

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:30.049
You're not one
of those guys, though,

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.318
that is bare feet on an airplane
that does this thing.

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:33.519
-No, absolutely not.
-Okay.

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:35.088
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
I would never.

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:36.089
-No, you wouldn't.

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:39.224
-My feet are not
meant to be seen.

00:02:39.224 --> 00:02:40.727
-I see.
-Yeah.

00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:42.895
-Yeah. Mine aren't usually,
but I didn't have

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:44.997
any good shoes
to go with this outfit, so...

00:02:44.997 --> 00:02:47.933
-What was -- What inspired you
to write this book?

00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:51.003
It seems like maybe the pandemic
was a tipping point to --

00:02:51.003 --> 00:02:52.805
It's not your first book.
You've written others.

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:54.340
-I have. It was.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.443
I got COVID
very early on, in March,

00:02:57.443 --> 00:03:01.814
back when the only photos
of this global pandemic

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:03.749
were people on a ventilator,
so it was a little scary.

00:03:03.749 --> 00:03:05.818
-It was a very scary time.
-It was.

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:09.188
And my husband,
George Stephanopoulos --

00:03:09.188 --> 00:03:13.326
-They should say his name
like that on the news,

00:03:13.326 --> 00:03:14.594
like, when they introduce him.

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:18.031
-"This week with
George Stephanopoulos."

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:21.467
-Yeah.

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:28.207
-Oh, I'm going to be
divorced tomorrow.

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:30.976
-Yeah.
-So, I got it early on.

00:03:30.976 --> 00:03:33.913
We actually took a picture
that we put on "GMA"

00:03:33.913 --> 00:03:37.183
just to basically say, you know,
"I know we're all terrified.

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:39.152
Ali's actually at home."

00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:42.388
So back then,
when you quarantined,

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:44.190
I quarantined for three weeks

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:49.862
in a little room,
and it got kind of gross.

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:53.432
But I could hear
my daughters downstairs.

00:03:53.432 --> 00:03:54.700
And I thought to myself,

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:58.371
"How am I going to
grow teenagers in a pandemic?"

00:03:58.371 --> 00:04:00.072
You know,
they're supposed to be out

00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:01.774
robbing liquor stores
and getting knocked up.

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:03.175
-Yeah.

00:04:03.175 --> 00:04:07.613
-And, instead, they're just
in the house with me and George,

00:04:07.613 --> 00:04:09.048
and that's -- that is going to
stunt their growth.

00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:11.517
-That's not living.
-That's not --

00:04:13.352 --> 00:04:15.521
They're Stephanopoulos girls.

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:19.725
So, I was thinking about
the book, and I thought,

00:04:19.725 --> 00:04:21.494
"I'm going to write a book,
originally,

00:04:21.494 --> 00:04:25.231
about how do we help
our children through this?"

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:27.266
That was sort of
the impetus for it.

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:30.269
And then what really got me
to write the book was,

00:04:30.269 --> 00:04:33.239
I had such a high fever
for a few days

00:04:33.239 --> 00:04:35.741
and I watched all, you know,

00:04:35.741 --> 00:04:39.011
5,000 seasons of "Mad Men,"
but, like, on a loop.

00:04:39.011 --> 00:04:42.081
Like, the next episode would
just automatically start.

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:43.750
So for about
three days straight,

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:46.219
I watched "Mad Men,"
and I was so delirious

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:49.522
in my fever that I --
This is real.

00:04:49.522 --> 00:04:52.691
I thought I was
married to Don Draper.

00:04:52.691 --> 00:04:56.095
-Okay, yeah.
-And he was cheating on me.

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:59.999
So, George would come and, like,
all masked up and, like,

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:04.837
put my tray in with some kind of
weird broth he cooked up.

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:07.072
And I would scream at him like,

00:05:07.072 --> 00:05:09.475
"I know you're sleeping with
that French secretary!"

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:11.677
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:11.677 --> 00:05:15.414
-And George's reaction was like,
"Oh, it's just crazy Ali."

00:05:15.414 --> 00:05:18.217
-Yeah, yeah.
-And it got worse and worse.

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:21.520
And then, at one point,
he came in again

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.223
with the mask and the gloves to
bring me some sad

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:25.724
peanut-butter-and-jelly
sandwich.

00:05:25.724 --> 00:05:27.559
And he was like,
"You know, I love you,

00:05:27.559 --> 00:05:28.895
and I think
you're getting better.

00:05:28.895 --> 00:05:30.996
Your oxygen levels are,
you know, higher."

00:05:30.996 --> 00:05:32.298
And I was like, "I'm gonna --

00:05:32.298 --> 00:05:35.367
I am taking my percentage of
the Draper Industry."

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:38.938
You know. And, so, that's what
scared George the most --

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:42.474
not that I had COVID, but that I
firmly believed that he --

00:05:42.474 --> 00:05:44.643
-Because everybody says
loss of smell is bad,

00:05:44.643 --> 00:05:47.446
but I think thinking
you're Betty Draper is worse...

00:05:47.446 --> 00:05:49.415
-Oh, yeah.
-...as a symptom.

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:50.716
-It was.
-Yeah.

00:05:50.716 --> 00:05:55.321
-The only good part about
that delirium dream was that

00:05:55.321 --> 00:06:00.192
I had huge knockers and I was
drinking a lot of martinis.

00:06:00.192 --> 00:06:01.960
And there was a fun part of it,

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:04.563
but it took me
a while, even today,

00:06:04.563 --> 00:06:07.466
to realize that George
is not sleeping around.

00:06:07.466 --> 00:06:08.601
Or maybe he is.

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:10.035
Maybe you know
something I don't.

00:06:10.035 --> 00:06:12.271
-Well, you did, like a lot --

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:14.039
You did have him at home
during the pandemic.

00:06:14.039 --> 00:06:15.808
He was doing "GMA"
from the house.

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:18.577
I -- You know,
I think that maybe too much

00:06:18.577 --> 00:06:21.113
has been put on the people
who are doing the shows.

00:06:21.113 --> 00:06:22.581
It must have been a lot to
live with people

00:06:22.581 --> 00:06:23.715
who are doing a show.

00:06:23.715 --> 00:06:25.418
-Yes. Thank you.
-You're welcome.

00:06:25.418 --> 00:06:27.353
-Thank you for -- Thank you.

00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:29.455
I mean, yes,
he pays the mortgage,

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:32.925
but it's also
really hard for me.

00:06:32.925 --> 00:06:36.796
So, during COVID,
George Stephanopoulos

00:06:36.796 --> 00:06:42.101
was doing "GMA"
from our dining room,

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:44.770
which is just
incredibly difficult,

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:47.172
because you have
a wife upstairs screaming,

00:06:47.172 --> 00:06:48.173
"I know you're cheating on me."

00:06:48.173 --> 00:06:49.475
-Yeah, that's not great.

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:51.910
-And you have
two teenage daughters

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:54.547
that are also like,
"Daddy stole my makeup,"

00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:57.583
because there's no makeup artist
in our dining room.

00:06:57.583 --> 00:07:01.287
And we have two very yappy dogs
that if you even fart,

00:07:01.287 --> 00:07:03.289
they start barking like crazy.

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:07.960
So, he's -- you know, has his
boxers and then a jacket and tie

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.596
with, like,
the ABC logo behind him.

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:12.765
And he's got a handful of
treats, and any --

00:07:12.765 --> 00:07:14.100
You know, they go like,
"Breaking news."

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.801
[ Vocalizes dramatic music ]
"ABC breaking news."

00:07:15.801 --> 00:07:18.337
He was so afraid the dogs
were going to start barking

00:07:18.337 --> 00:07:22.274
that he just kept throwing
treats during all of "GMA."

00:07:22.274 --> 00:07:24.443
So, that man was a rock star.

00:07:24.443 --> 00:07:25.844
I mean,
the plates he was juggling --

00:07:25.844 --> 00:07:30.883
-So after, like, a big news
week, were your dog's super-fat?

00:07:30.883 --> 00:07:33.319
-Well, our dachshund
is morbidly obese.

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:34.653
-Yeah.

00:07:34.653 --> 00:07:38.957
-And, by the way, I packed on
some pounds during COVID,

00:07:38.957 --> 00:07:42.828
as -- By the way, a lot of data
says that women --

00:07:42.828 --> 00:07:45.798
we internalized
and we ate and got heavy,

00:07:45.798 --> 00:07:48.100
and men were like,
"I'm going to go use this time

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:52.004
to go bike 5,000 miles,"
so they all got in shape.

00:07:52.004 --> 00:07:56.375
I went to see my doctor after
the two years of quarantine

00:07:56.375 --> 00:07:58.110
and eating a lot of
Fudgie the Whale cakes,

00:07:58.110 --> 00:07:59.445
which is the cover of my book,

00:07:59.445 --> 00:08:01.380
because that was
our coping mechanism.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:05.051
And my doctor called me back
at 10:30 at night,

00:08:05.051 --> 00:08:07.152
and I said, "Hi, Dr. Kurth."

00:08:07.152 --> 00:08:10.055
And she's like, "Ali,
your cholesterol is 336."

00:08:10.055 --> 00:08:12.357
And I was like,
"Yes! What does that mean?"

00:08:12.357 --> 00:08:16.662
And she goes like,
"You're going to die.

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:19.765
So what have you been
eating during quarantine?"

00:08:19.765 --> 00:08:22.401
I said, "Well, like, what
everybody else has been eating,

00:08:22.401 --> 00:08:25.705
a baguette dipped
in cheese and ice cream."

00:08:25.705 --> 00:08:28.474
And she was like,
"Yeah, no more, no more."

00:08:28.474 --> 00:08:32.511
I still am,
but I'm taking a statin.

00:08:32.511 --> 00:08:36.215
-I think everyone --

00:08:36.215 --> 00:08:37.683
Based on what
you've said tonight,

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:40.919
everyone will want to
read even more about

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:42.721
Ali's trip through a pandemic.

00:08:42.721 --> 00:08:44.022
Ali Wentworth, everybody.

00:08:44.022 --> 00:08:46.125
"Ali's Well That Ends Well"
is available May 10th.

00:08:46.125 --> 00:08:47.727
We'll be right back with more
"Late Night."

